    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "THE TWO DIRECTIONS DO NOT REST ON THE SAME THING, and the difference is worth stating because it is the opposite of the usual asymmetry. A LOSSY verdict rests on no theorem at all: the certificate is a relation that satisfies F, whose projections demonstrably rejoin to a tuple it does not contain, and the three obligations are decided by comparing arrays. A LOSSLESS verdict rests on the soundness of the chase (Aho, Beeri and Ullman 1979) -- replaying the identifications to an all-distinguished row means the join is lossless only because that theorem says so. So the negative half of this fact is strictly better supported than the positive half, and the axiom footprint says which is which.\n\nWHY THE `naive` SPLIT IS IN THE INSTANCE. Splitting street/city/zip on `city` is the decomposition a designer reaches for when they see `zip -> city` and think in terms of lookup tables. It loses information outright, and the run prints the two-row database that proves it. A tool that only ever said `not in BCNF` would give that designer no way to tell the two repairs apart.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
